## Amethystora audit rules: what an attacker has to touch to survive a reboot or to gain root.
##
## The image itself needs no watching. /usr is read only and every update replaces it, so nothing
## written there lasts. What does last is /etc, each user's home directory, and the running kernel,
## and that is what these rules cover. Watches on the per-user paths are generated separately, into
## 61-amethystora-home.rules by /usr/libexec/amethystora-audit-home-rules, because an audit watch
## needs a path that already exists when the rules are loaded.
##
## The security watcher (/usr/libexec/amethystora-security-watch) reads what they caught and says so;
## `ame security events` shows it. By hand: sudo ausearch -k <key> -i
## Keys used below: identity, privilege, persistence, hardening, modules, input-capture. A new key has to
## be added to the watcher and to amethystora-security-realtime as well.

## Accounts, and who is allowed to become root
-w /etc/passwd -p wa -k identity
-w /etc/shadow -p wa -k identity
-w /etc/group -p wa -k identity
-w /etc/gshadow -p wa -k identity
-w /etc/sudoers -p wa -k privilege
-w /etc/sudoers.d/ -p wa -k privilege
-w /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/ -p wa -k privilege
-w /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/ -p wa -k privilege

## Anything that starts itself, in the writable parts of the system. Quadlets are containers systemd
## starts at boot.
-w /etc/systemd/system/ -p wa -k persistence
-w /etc/xdg/autostart/ -p wa -k persistence
-w /etc/profile.d/ -p wa -k persistence
-w /etc/containers/systemd/ -p wa -k persistence

## The hardening itself. Whatever turns these off does it before doing anything else worth seeing.
-w /etc/flatpak/overrides/ -p wa -k hardening
## Firefox's policy: where browser protection is written, and where an extension can be forced in
-w /etc/firefox/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/containers/policy.json -p wa -k hardening
## The key that policy.json names, and where the signatures it demands are looked up. Swapping the key
## for one of the attacker's own defeats update verification with policy.json left untouched, so a
## watch on that file alone watches the lock and not the key. The akmods certificate is the same
## question one layer down: it is what says a kernel module was built by this image.
-w /etc/pki/containers/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/containers/registries.d/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/pki/akmods/certs/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/security/faillock.conf -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/audit/ -p wa -k hardening
## Where a file of the same name as one in /usr/lib replaces it whole, which turns the image's kernel
## settings, blocked modules or firewall zone off without touching /usr; how passwords are checked; the
## SSH server; what the virus scan leaves out; and this machine's own security settings.
-w /etc/sysctl.d/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/modprobe.d/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/firewalld/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/pam.d/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/authselect/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/clamd.d/ -p wa -k hardening
-w /etc/amethystora/ -p wa -k hardening

## Loading a kernel module, by anyone who logged in. Modules the system loads for itself during boot
## are not attributable to a login (auid is unset) and would otherwise fill the log.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S init_module,finit_module,delete_module -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k modules

## Opening an input device: the one system call a software keylogger cannot avoid making. GNOME does
## not appear here, because logind opens input devices on the session's behalf and passes the file
## descriptors in, as root and without a login id. A program the user runs does appear.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S openat -F dir=/dev/input -F success=1 -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k input-capture
